%T A400335 12458784,55415816,253142182,1187979372,5712033368,28131357330,
%U A400335 141645386202,728842555928,3827217750492,20499438267488,111876916526070,621831376408274,3516904353610572,20230575916118016
%N A400335 Number of partial matchings of n points on a circle up to rotation.
%C A400335 Equivalently, a(n) is the number of orbits of the involutions p of Z_n = {0, 1, ..., n-1} (permutations with p(p(i)) = i for all i) under conjugation by the cyclic shift i -> i+1; i.e., the number of partial chord diagrams on a necklace of n points, where points may be left unmatched and chords may cross.
%C A400335 a(n) is also the number of side-pairing patterns of an n-gon up to rotation of the n-gon: each side is either glued to itself by a half-turn about its midpoint (a fixed point of p) or glued to one other side by an orientation-preserving map (a 2-cycle of p). By Poincare's polygon theorem, these patterns describe the tilings of the sphere, the Euclidean plane or the hyperbolic plane by copies of one n-gon on which a group of orientation-preserving isometries acts simply transitively on the tiles.
%C A400335 Perfect matchings (n even, no fixed points) up to rotation are counted by A007769(n/2); partial matchings up to rotation and reflection are counted by A400336.
%H A400335 Rasmus Joergensen, <a href="/A400335/b400335.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..200</a>
%H A400335 Rasmus Joergensen, <a href="https://www.transpositional.org/n-colourings/">Tables and pictures of the patterns for n = 2..10</a>
%F A400335 a(n) = (1/n)*Sum_{d|n} phi(n/d)*F(d, n/d), where F(g, L) = Sum_{k=0..floor(g/2)} g!/((g-2*k)!*k!*2^k) * L^k * s^(g-2*k), with s = 2 if L is even and s = 1 if L is odd.
%F A400335 a(n) = (1/n)*Sum_{r=0..n-1} F(gcd(n,r), n/gcd(n,r)).
%F A400335 F(g, L) is the number of involutions commuting with a permutation that has g cycles of length L; F(g, L) = g! * [x^g] exp(s*x + L*x^2/2), F(g, 1) = A000085(g), F(g, 2) = A000898(g).
%F A400335 a(p) = (A000085(p) + p - 1)/p for odd primes p.
%e A400335 a(4) = 5: writing the involutions of Z_4 = {0,1,2,3} as products of transpositions, representatives of the 5 classes are (), (0 1), (0 2), (0 1)(2 3), (0 2)(1 3); the 10 involutions of Z_4 fall into classes of sizes 1, 4, 2, 2, 1.
%o A400335 (Python)
%o A400335 from math import gcd, factorial
%o A400335 def F(g, L):
%o A400335     # involutions commuting with a permutation that has g cycles of length L
%o A400335     s = 2 if L % 2 == 0 else 1
%o A400335     return sum(factorial(g)//(factorial(g-2*p)*factorial(p)*2**p)*L**p*s**(g-2*p) for p in range(g//2+1))
%o A400335 def a(n):
%o A400335     return sum(F(gcd(n, r), n//gcd(n, r)) for r in range(n))//n
%Y A400335 Cf. A000085, A000898, A007769, A054499, A061417, A400336.
%K A400335 nonn,new
%O A400335 1,2
%A A400335 _Rasmus Joergensen_, Sep 23 2026
